  "structured": true,
  "speaker": "Abhinavagupta (commentator voice)",
  "question": "The Buddhist's pūrvapakṣa (V1-F..J) has denied the self, the cognition, the action,\nand the relation. Now, in the third vimarśa, the siddhānta replies. Abhinavagupta grants the Buddhist\nmuch — that the cognition is not a separate thing in the Sāṃkhya/Kāṇāda views — but he turns it to his\nown end: the cognition is *self-cognition* (svasaṃvid), and this self-cognition is precisely the self\nthat the Buddhist denied. The \"I\"-seeing (dṛk) is self-luminous, and it is the Lord.",
  "premises": [
   "\"Bowing to the will-power-full, nectar-shining, capable of generating endless worlds; the Bhairava-joy,\nthickened by the waves of the own-power…\" The siddhānta is established in two adhikāras. In seven\nverses (from \"satyaṃ kintu\" to \"smṛtyapohanaśaktimān\"), Abhinava examines and refutes the other view\n(paradarśanānupapatti). The jñāna-vicāra is this adhikāra (the action-relation in the second). The plan:\ntwo verses show the cognition is self-cognition (in memory, the experience does not shine, even in the\nimpression-raised); two reject the memory's error-ness (with a doubt), and by the third the\nconstruction's error-ness is rejected by the prasaṅga (all the determinations); one shows that even\nwith the impression, in the other view, memory is unfitting (the dealing-lapse); one establishes it in\nour view. The \"na\" is used where the pūrvapakṣa is wholly to be stopped; the \"satyaṃ kintu\" where\npartly to be accepted and partly rejected. This is the style of the prekṣāvat (the reflective) ones.\nHere \"satyaṃ\" (true) grants much of the pūrvapakṣa: the \"in the Sāṃkhya/Kāṇāda view the cognition is\nnot established\" — that is agreed. But the \"kintu\" (but) shows the further distinction: what the Śākya\nmeant is to be sharpened."
  ],
  "inference": "Decision for L2\nProceed. The argument is understood: the siddhānta grants the Buddhist that the cognition is not a\nseparate thing, but turns it around — the cognition is self-cognition, the self-luminous \"I\"-seeing,\nwhich is the very self the Buddhist denied. This is the self-luminous dṛk, the Lord. The jñāna-vicāra\nbegins the reply.\nEOF\necho \"V1K map: $(wc -l < /mnt/HC_Volume_106427611/sanskritree/translations/_stack/ipvv/pilot/pilot_V1K_ARGUMENT_MAP.md) lines\"",
  "uncertainties": [
   "The \"satyaṃ kintu\" concessive style (the prekṣāvat) — Abhinava's rhetorical method; preserved.",
   "The \"smṛtyapohanaśaktimān\" (the one possessed of the memory-removal-power) — the siddhānta's"
  ],
